@@ -92,6 +92,8 @@ Bugs
 - The Castillos2023 c-VEP datasets (:class:`moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP40`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP40`) now return the same 28 EEG channels, in one order, for every subject. The released files use two 32-channel layouts: subjects 1, 5, 6, 7, 11 and 12 carry the LiveAmp-32 labels (F9, P9, P10, F10) and subjects 2, 3, 4, 8, 9 and 10 the actiCAP-32 labels (FT9, TP9, TP10, FT10), in a different channel order, so arrays mixing the two groups stacked different electrodes at the same index (e.g. index 9 was CP5 for subject 1 and TP9 for subject 2) and ``return_epochs=True`` failed with "epochs[1].info['ch_names'] must match". The four layout-specific electrodes are dropped; ``METADATA`` and the summary table list 28 channels. Cached Castillos2023 epochs and saved results must be deleted and recomputed (:gh:`XXXX` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
 - The Castillos2023 c-VEP datasets (:class:`moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP40`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP40`) now return the same 28 EEG channels, in one order, for every subject. The released files use two 32-channel layouts: subjects 1, 5, 6, 7, 11 and 12 carry the LiveAmp-32 labels (F9, P9, P10, F10) and subjects 2, 3, 4, 8, 9 and 10 the actiCAP-32 labels (FT9, TP9, TP10, FT10), in a different channel order, so arrays mixing the two groups stacked different electrodes at the same index (e.g. index 9 was CP5 for subject 1 and TP9 for subject 2) and ``return_epochs=True`` failed with "epochs[1].info['ch_names'] must match". The four layout-specific electrodes are dropped; ``METADATA`` and the summary table list 28 channels. Cached Castillos2023 epochs and saved results must be deleted and recomputed (:gh:`1282` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
 - Fix the Castillos2023 c-VEP loaders (:class:`moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosBurstVEP40`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP100`, :class:`~moabb.datasets.CastillosCVEP40`) failing for every subject with ``ValueError: The event_id dictionary has values that do not match any event codes ... Missing event codes: [0]``: the trial labels were a view of ``epochs.events``, so making them 0-based also rewrote the epochs' event codes, which MNE now checks when the epochs are read. The labels are now a copy, and all 12 subjects of the four datasets load again (:gh:`1279` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
+- Fix :class:`moabb.datasets.PhysionetMI` failing in every paradigm call that mixes subjects 88, 92 or 100 with other subjects: these three subjects were recorded at 128 Hz (every imagery and execution run) and the other 106 at 160 Hz, so ``LeftRightImagery().get_data(PhysionetMI(), subjects=[1, 88])`` stopped in ``np.concatenate`` ("the array at index 0 has size 481 and the array at index 6 has size 385") and ``return_epochs=True`` in ``concatenate_epochs`` ("epochs[6].info['sfreq'] must match"); the docstring only mentioned subject 88. The loader now resamples any run that is not at 160 Hz to 160 Hz, so all 109 subjects load together. Cached epochs and saved results that include subjects 88, 92 or 100 must be deleted and recomputed (:gh:`XXXX` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
+- Fix :class:`moabb.datasets.PhysionetMI` failing in every paradigm call that mixes subjects 88, 92 or 100 with other subjects: these three subjects were recorded at 128 Hz (every imagery and execution run) and the other 106 at 160 Hz, so ``LeftRightImagery().get_data(PhysionetMI(), subjects=[1, 88])`` stopped in ``np.concatenate`` ("the array at index 0 has size 481 and the array at index 6 has size 385") and ``return_epochs=True`` in ``concatenate_epochs`` ("epochs[6].info['sfreq'] must match"); the docstring only mentioned subject 88. The loader now resamples any run that is not at 160 Hz to 160 Hz, so all 109 subjects load together. Cached epochs and saved results that include subjects 88, 92 or 100 must be deleted and recomputed (:gh:`1280` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
 - :class:`moabb.datasets.Liu2024` no longer loads subjects 5, 15 and 49 by default: their released files are derived from the recordings of subjects 4, 11 and 47 (EDF of sub-05 bit-identical to sub-04; raw MAT files equal to the partner's recording minus uniform noise), while ``participants.tsv`` lists them as different patients, so keeping them leaked one subject's EEG across cross-subject folds. The dataset now exposes 47 subjects; ``include_derived_subjects=True`` restores all 50 released IDs with a warning. Scores computed on all 50 subjects are not comparable with the new default (:gh:`1248` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
 - Follow-ups from the review of :gh:`1257`-:gh:`1270`: :func:`moabb.benchmark` lets the evaluation auto-select datasets when no ``include_datasets``/``exclude_datasets`` is given, so its default run no longer warns about filtered datasets (:gh:`862`), and "No datasets left" now lists the reasons; the weekly scheduled link check no longer accepts 5xx answers, so a host that stays down is reported; the PhysioNet S3 mirror is skipped for the rest of the process after its first failure instead of warning and timing out once per file (:gh:`1245`); a metadata-aware ``cv_class`` now rejects a ``groups`` argument it would silently ignore, and the CrossSession limitation is documented (:gh:`1104`); a Zhou2020 file that does not match the channel layout raises instead of being skipped (:gh:`1120`); the Rozado2015 unit check accepts common spellings of microvolts (:gh:`1070`); ``Lioi2020_XP2.nemar_subject_template`` is read-only and a missing mapping key names the expected key type (:gh:`1098`); the ``process()`` Notes state that saving finished subjects is best effort with ``n_jobs > 1`` (:gh:`419`) (by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
 - With ``optuna=True``, ROC-AUC scoring failed with ``AttributeError: ... has no attribute 'decision_function'`` for classifiers that only provide ``predict_proba`` (skorch/braindecode nets, ``GaussianNB``, ...): optuna-integration's ``OptunaSearchCV`` defines ``decision_function`` unconditionally, so scikit-learn's scorer picked it. ``OptunaSearchCVClassifier`` now exposes it only when the underlying estimator has it, as scikit-learn's ``BaseSearchCV`` does (:gh:`1251` by `Bruno Aristimunha`_).
